content.reasoning:
A 422 invalid_enum on severity "low"?! But "low" is in the allowed list [low, medium, high]. That's bizarre — same error as my very first attempt. Wait... my first attempt with the wrapped array also got this exact error, and then the single-object {severity: high, note} worked.

Why would {severity: "low", note: "..."} fail with invalid_enum? Unless the validation isn't on my input's value but on something else... Maybe the error is misleading and it's actually a STATE-based rejection: e.g., the work order only accepts certain severities now, or... hmm.

Actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the 422 is a red herring: perhaps there's a per-request validation quirk. Let me retry the exact same call once? Blind retrying isn't great, but the error is factually wrong ("low" is allowed). 

Hmm, alternatively the API might reject "low" for this work order because the work order (or inspection) only needs high/medium — but then the error would say so.

Interesting parallel: my very first findings POST (wrapped array) got the same 422 invalid_enum + "note is also required". Then single object worked. The error message seems to be a generic validation failure message. Maybe the actual validation logic: body must be a single finding object with severity in {low,medium,high} AND note present. My low attempt satisfies both... unless there's something about the exact payload. Let me compare:

Worked: {"severity": "high", "note": "Brake pad thickness below minimum on axle 2"}
Worked: {"severity": "medium", "note": "Brake hose chafing at bogie frame"}
Failed: {"severity": "low", "note": "Cab wiper blade perished — monitor"}

The em-dash "—" in the note! Maybe the API has an issue with non-ASCII characters in note, and the validation error is misattributed to severity. Let me retry with plain ASCII.
